{"sounds":{"intro":"Jamaican Patois (Patwa) is an English-based creole spoken by almost everyone in Jamaica. It has no single official spelling. This pack uses a simple, widely used spelling close to the Cassidy-JLU system, with a plain-English respelling in the \"say\" line. It is the language of reggae, Bob Marley and Jamaican street life, and it is a language, not \"broken English\".","rows":[["th","\"t\" or \"d\": \"think\" is \"tink\", \"this\" is \"dis\"","tink"],["h","often dropped: \"hand\" is \"han\"","han"],["-ing","the ending becomes \"-in\" or is dropped: \"going\" is \"a go\"","a go"],["w / y","\"wah\" for \"what\", \"weh\" for \"where\"","wah"]],"notes":["Jamaican Patois is spoken in a continuum from near-standard English to heavy dialect. This pack is the middle and heavier end. Spellings vary a lot between writers.","This pool is much smaller than the Spanish one on purpose and has not been checked by a native speaker."]},"numbers":[{"id":"1-to-10","title":"1 to 10","items":[["one","wan","wun"],["two","tuu","too"],["three","trii","tree"],["four","fuor","for"],["five","faiv","five"],["six","siks","siks"],["seven","sevn","SEV-n"],["eight","ayt","ate"],["nine","nain","nine"],["ten","ten","ten"]]}],"greetings":[{"id":"greetings-and-politeness","title":"Greetings and politeness","items":[["hello / what's going on?","Wah gwaan?","wah GWAHN"],["I'm fine","Mi deh yah","mee deh YAH"],["good morning","Mawnin","MAW-nin"],["good evening","Gud evening","gud EEV-nin"],["see you later","Likkle more","LIK-l more"],["no problem","Nuh problem","nuh PROB-lem"],["thank you","Tenk yuh","tenk yuh"],["please","Pliiz","pleez"],["sorry","Sorry","SOR-ee"],["yes","Yes","yes"],["no","Nuh","nuh"],["everything is fine","Irie","EYE-ree"]]}],"phrases":[{"id":"about-me","title":"About me","items":[["I","mi","mee"],["you","yuh","yuh"],["he / she / it","im / shi / it","eem / shee / it"],["we","wi","wee"],["they","dem","dem"],["my name is Eric","Mi niem Eric","mee NYAME Eric"],["what's your name?","Wah yuh niem?","wah yuh NYAME"],["I am a student","Mi a student","mee a STOO-dent"],["I am a teacher","Mi a tiicha","mee a TEE-cha"],["I want food","Mi waan nyam","mee wahn NYAM"],["where are you going?","Weh yuh a go?","weh yuh a GO"],["I am going home","Mi a go yaad","mee a go YAAD"],["I don't know","Mi nuh know","mee nuh NOH"],["I know","Mi know","mee NOH"],["I can't go","Mi cyaan go","mee CHAN go"],["I did not see him","Mi neva si im","mee NEV-a see eem"]]}],"vocab":[{"id":"people-and-family","title":"People and family","items":[["child","pickney","PIK-nee"],["friend","fren / bredda","fren / BRED-a"],["man","man","man"],["woman","uman","OO-man"],["boy","bwoy","boy"],["girl","gyal","gyal"],["mother","mada","MAH-da"],["father","fada","FAH-da"]]},{"id":"school-and-everyday","title":"School and everyday","items":[["school","skool","skool"],["teacher","tiicha","TEE-cha"],["book","buk","buk"],["to learn","laan","laan"],["house / home","yaad","yaad"],["water","wata","WAH-ta"],["food","food","food"],["to eat","nyam","nyam"],["to drink","drink","drink"],["to go","go","go"],["to come","kom","kom"],["to see","si","see"],["to give","gi","gee"],["to say","se","say"]]},{"id":"describing","title":"Describing","items":[["big","big","big"],["small","likkle","LIK-l"],["good","good","good"],["bad","bad","bad"],["nice / sweet","sweet","sweet"],["very","real / fi true","real / fee TROO"]]}],"grammar":[{"id":"pronouns","title":"Pronouns","text":["Patois pronouns do not change for subject or object, except he/she/it (im, shi, it). Plural is \"dem\" after the noun: \"di pickney dem\" (the children)."],"rows":[["I / me","mi","mee"],["you","yuh","yuh"],["he / she / him / her","im / shi","eem / shee"],["we","wi","wee"],["they","dem","dem"]],"ex":[["I am a student","Mi a student","mee a STOO-dent"],["we are friends","Wi a fren dem","wee a fren dem"],["they are teachers","Dem a tiicha dem","dem a TEE-cha dem"]]},{"id":"verbs-and-tenses","title":"Verbs and tenses","text":["No -ing and no past endings. \"A\" marks something happening now: \"Mi a go\". \"Did\" marks past: \"Mi did go\". \"Go\" marks future: \"Mi a go kom\" (I will come)."],"rows":[["I go","Mi go","mee go"],["I am going","Mi a go","mee a go"],["I went","Mi did go","mee did go"]],"ex":[["I eat","Mi nyam","mee nyam"],["I am eating","Mi a nyam","mee a nyam"],["I ate","Mi did nyam","mee did nyam"]]},{"id":"negation","title":"Negation","text":["Put \"nuh\" before the verb or noun. For the past, use \"neva\". For \"can't\", use \"cyaan\"."],"rows":[["not","nuh","nuh"],["never / did not","neva","NEV-a"],["can't","cyaan","chan"]],"ex":[["I know","Mi know","mee noh"],["I don't know","Mi nuh know","mee nuh noh"],["I saw him","Mi si im","mee see eem"],["I did not see him","Mi neva si im","mee NEV-a see eem"]]}],"name":"Jamaican Patois","id":"jam","flag":"jam","note":""}